save('23-mss-action-uncertainty.md','''Intended mind change: Choose a model of why unresolved evidence sometimes blocks an action and sometimes does not, using a finite declared decision problem.

# MSS 03 — uncertainty and action thresholds

Input: a quantity x is only known to lie in [4,9]. The stipulated action rule is choose A when x is at least threshold t, otherwise B. No probabilities over x are supplied. Starting judgment: at t=6 the evidence is insufficient for one determinate action; at t=12 it selects B throughout the interval. The unresolved question is whether a single confidence score is an adequate explanation of this difference.

Original MSS/receipt preserved; no numerical 8x definition. Six models include null, causal, mechanistic, and analogical candidates; all seven original scoring criteria are applied. Observations: x’s interval is fixed, threshold varies; t=4 selects A throughout, t=6 splits, t=9 splits at the endpoint, t=12 selects B throughout.

| Model | Mechanism / prediction | Fit | No contradiction | Predictive | Simple | Generality | Coherent | Mechanism | Total |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| M0 Confidence alone controls action | Same uncertainty means same readiness for all t | 2 | 2 | 2 | 9 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 27 |
| M1 Threshold sensitivity | Decision changes only where candidate x values imply different actions | 10 | 10 | 10 | 9 | 7 | 10 | 10 | 66 |
| M2 Midpoint imputation | Replace interval by 6.5 and decide | 5 | 5 | 5 | 10 | 4 | 6 | 8 | 43 |
| M3 Worst-case policy | Choose an act by the least favorable endpoint | 6 | 7 | 6 | 7 | 6 | 7 | 7 | 46 |
| M4 Missing probability distribution | No expected utility is computable, so all actions are blocked | 3 | 3 | 3 | 7 | 4 | 5 | 5 | 30 |
| M5 Partition analogy | A boundary partitions the possible-state set; action is constant within each cell | 10 | 10 | 9 | 8 | 7 | 10 | 9 | 63 |

M1 describes the declared deterministic rule without adding a distribution or risk preference. M2 changes the input by imputing a value. M3 could be appropriate under a separately declared loss rule; it is not implied by the given threshold function. M4 correctly identifies absent probabilities but falsely requires them when every admissible x yields the same action. M5 is a structural analogy to M1, not independent evidence.

Later use: the model retains the same [4,9] interval in three decisions. It chooses A at t=4, preserves unresolved choice at t=6, and chooses B at t=12. Thus “increase confidence” is not selected as a necessary preliminary for all three. The user’s real utilities and probabilities are not inferred from this stipulated example.

Certificate: the action set {f(x,t):x in [4,9]} has cardinality one at t=4 and 12 and two at t=6. This follows directly from the declared inequalities. Strong contrary branch is a different loss function or action set, requiring a fresh model. Confidence in the finite result is high; transfer to human decision making is untested.

Actual mind change: No new conceptual result beyond DD’s threshold distinction; the model now executes that distinction on declared values.
Benefit: Warranted uncertainty is preserved while two determinate actions proceed. This is transfer/use of an existing keep, not independent new credit.
Verdict: UNRESOLVED — no new keep; real-world decision effects untested.
Organization: Evidence interval, action rule, and optional risk policy are separated; the model does not invent a probability distribution.
Next attempts: Add asymmetric losses explicitly; test discontinuous or multi-action rules; use a held-out interval; examine a case where observation cost exceeds the value of resolving the split.
''')
